},"gro-best-1":{"a":"Best product for this need: Harvest Grain Flakes | Strongest evidence: Harvest Grain Flakes lists a whole grain first and has 5 g fiber and 4 g added sugars per serving, matching all three requirements.","d":"labels","c":"product-compare","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"grocery comparison: choice + evidence","p":3,"m":"m2","s":"gro-1","sl":"gro-best","mj":1,"h":["ef482e90399db6fd1013"],"x":"The best match depends on this specific need. Harvest Grain Flakes lists a whole grain first and has 5 g fiber and 4 g added sugars per serving, matching all three requirements. Front-of-package claims do not show whether a product meets the requirements."},"gro-rank-1":{"a":"1. Protein Power Puffs | 2. Harvest Grain Flakes | 3. Berry Blast Crunch","d":"labels","c":"added-sugar-names","k":"rank","df":2,"y":"seq","qt":"grocery comparison: ranking","p":1,"m":"m2","s":"gro-1","sl":"gro-rank","mj":0,"h":["272651ab27ca275edbac"],"x":"Protein Power Puffs has 0 g, Harvest Grain Flakes has 4 g, and Berry Blast Crunch has 14 g added sugars per serving."},"gro-best-2":{"a":"Best product for this need: Vanilla Greek Yogurt Cup | Strongest evidence: Vanilla Greek Yogurt Cup has 15 g protein, 15% DV calcium and 6 g added sugars per serving, which meets all three requirements.","d":"labels","c":"product-compare","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"grocery comparison: choice + evidence","p":3,"m":"m2","s":"gro-2","sl":"gro-best","mj":1,"h":["44f8be9e3f27f8c3e0bf"],"x":"The best match depends on this specific need. Vanilla Greek Yogurt Cup has 15 g protein, 15% DV calcium and 6 g added sugars per serving, which meets all three requirements. Front-of-package claims do not show whether a product meets the requirements."},"gro-rank-2":{"a":"1. Vanilla Greek Yogurt Cup | 2. Choco Recovery Shake | 3. Tropic Glow Smoothie","d":"labels","c":"added-sugar-names","k":"rank","df":2,"y":"seq","qt":"grocery comparison: ranking","p":1,"m":"m2","s":"gro-2","sl":"gro-rank","mj":0,"h":["5ad90966960608978c21"],"x":"Vanilla Greek Yogurt Cup has 6 g, Choco Recovery Shake has 22 g, and Tropic Glow Smoothie has 36 g added sugars per serving."},"gro-best-3":{"a":"Best product for this need: Oat & Raisin Energy Bar | Strongest evidence: Oat & Raisin Energy Bar is nut-free and gives 220 calories per serving, which meets the need; Nutty Trail Bar contains nuts and Lite Berry Bar has only 90 calories.","d":"labels","c":"product-compare","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"grocery comparison: choice + evidence","p":3,"m":"m2","s":"gro-3","sl":"gro-best","mj":1,"h":["3bec095ed42abd511425"],"x":"The best match depends on this specific need. Oat & Raisin Energy Bar is nut-free and gives 220 calories per serving, which meets the need; Nutty Trail Bar contains nuts and Lite Berry Bar has only 90 calories. Front-of-package claims do not show whether a product meets the requirements."},"gro-rank-3":{"a":"1. Lite Berry Bar | 2. Nutty Trail Bar | 3. Oat & Raisin Energy Bar","d":"labels","c":"added-sugar-names","k":"rank","df":2,"y":"seq","qt":"grocery comparison: ranking","p":1,"m":"m2","s":"gro-3","sl":"gro-rank","mj":0,"h":["545fb4448d485450387c"],"x":"Lite Berry Bar has 6 g, Nutty Trail Bar has 9 g, and Oat & Raisin Energy Bar has 12 g added sugars per serving."},"a-fitt-id":{"a":"Adding 15 minutes to each walk → Time | Switching from walking to swimming → Type | Going from 3 days a week to 5 days a week → Frequency | Jogging at a pace where only a few words fit between breaths → Intensity","d":"activity","c":"fitt-variables","k":"classify","df":1,"y":"match","qt":"matching","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"a-fitt-id","sl":"a-fitt-id","mj":0,"h":["17a791c7aeedf9f4be16"],"x":"Adding minutes changes Time. Swimming instead of walking changes Type. More days changes Frequency. Working at a pace where talking is hard changes Intensity."},"a-apply-1":{"a":"Intensity","d":"activity","c":"fitt-apply","k":"diagnose","df":2,"y":"mc","qt":"scenario multiple choice","p":1,"m":"m3","s":"a-apply-1","sl":"a-apply","mj":0,"h":["758995f4351d5a102dc2"],"x":"The sessions already have a good Frequency and Time; the challenge is limited by how hard Jordan works, which is Intensity.","o":["f","in","t","ty"]},"a-apply-2":{"a":"Frequency","d":"activity","c":"fitt-apply","k":"diagnose","df":2,"y":"mc","qt":"scenario multiple choice","p":1,"m":"m3","s":"a-apply-2","sl":"a-apply","mj":0,"h":["970c73bb1d8434cef82c"],"x":"Priya's intensity, time and type are fine. Being active on more days means increasing Frequency.","o":["f","in","t","ty"]},"a-apply-3":{"a":"Time","d":"activity","c":"fitt-apply","k":"diagnose","df":2,"y":"mc","qt":"scenario multiple choice","p":1,"m":"m3","s":"a-apply-3","sl":"a-apply","mj":0,"h":["903af048a8d811ea41b4"],"x":"Frequency and Intensity are already in place. The ride length is short, so Time is what needs to increase.","o":["f","in","t","ty"]},"a-apply-4":{"a":"Type","d":"activity","c":"fitt-apply","k":"diagnose","df":2,"y":"mc","qt":"scenario multiple choice","p":1,"m":"m3","s":"a-apply-4","sl":"a-apply","mj":0,"h":["cdab5bf730204827bc80"],"x":"Running is aerobic. Adding muscle-strengthening activity means changing the Type of activity in the week.","o":["f","in","t","ty"]},"a-talk":{"a":"Maya jogs and can say only a few words between breaths → Vigorous | Leo cycles and can chat in full sentences but could not sing → Moderate | Ana walks her dog and hums along to her playlist → Light | Sam plays a fast basketball game and can answer only in single words → Vigorous | Kai power-walks; talking takes some effort, but full sentences are possible → Moderate | Ren stretches and could easily sing a whole song → Light","d":"activity","c":"intensity-talk","k":"classify","df":2,"y":"sort","qt":"simulation: drag-and-drop sort","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"intensity","sl":"a-talk","mj":1,"h":["802b34e9905424db63e3"],"x":"Light: you can sing or hum. Moderate: you can talk in sentences but not sing. Vigorous: you can say only a few words before needing a breath."},"a-conflict":{"a":"Talk test","d":"activity","c":"effort-scale","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"mc","qt":"simulation: evaluate conflicting signals","p":1,"m":"m3","s":"intensity","sl":"a-conflict","mj":1,"h":["27beb9cd65924698e30b"],"x":"Fast, deep breathing and a rating of 7 suggest vigorous effort, but being able to sing suggests light effort. The talk test does not match the other two signals, so the student should recheck how they are measuring their effort.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"a-rpe":{"a":"5 or 6","d":"activity","c":"effort-scale","k":"apply","df":2,"y":"slider","qt":"simulation: slider","p":1,"m":"m3","s":"intensity","sl":"a-rpe","mj":0,"h":["9567a9fb5458b92904ec","8e7ae358307cbd31a5af"],"x":"On the 0 to 10 scale, moderate-intensity activity is about 5 to 6, and vigorous is about 7 to 8."},"a-rec":{"a":"At least 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day | Vigorous-intensity activity on at least 3 days a week | Muscle-strengthening activity on at least 3 days a week | Bone-strengthening activity on at least 3 days a week","d":"activity","c":"activity-recs","k":"recall","df":2,"y":"multi","qt":"multi-select","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"a-rec","sl":"a-rec","mj":0,"h":["9cee0f1f2437f4a31bc6"],"x":"Teens should get at least 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity daily, including vigorous, muscle-strengthening and bone-strengthening activity on at least 3 days a week. The 150-minute target is for adults. Activity can be broken up, and any activity counts, not only organized sports."},"a-type":{"a":"Swimming laps → Aerobic only | Push-ups → Muscle-strengthening | Jump rope → Aerobic AND bone-strengthening (impact) | Cycling on a flat path → Aerobic only | Resistance-band rows → Muscle-strengthening | Basketball game → Aerobic AND bone-strengthening (impact)","d":"activity","c":"activity-types","k":"classify","df":2,"y":"match","qt":"matching","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"a-type","sl":"a-type","mj":0,"h":["74caf07e55b2155cf398"],"x":"Swimming and flat-path cycling raise heart rate but are low impact. Push-ups and band rows build muscle strength. Jump rope and basketball are aerobic and involve jumping and landing, which strengthens bones."},"a-plan":{"a":"All four checks met: 60+ moderate-to-vigorous minutes every day; vigorous on 3+ days; muscle-strengthening on 3+ days; bone-strengthening on 3+ days","d":"activity","c":"plan-build","k":"design","df":3,"y":"plan","qt":"FITT plan builder","p":3,"m":"m3","s":"a-plan","sl":"a-plan","mj":1,"h":["3753726ce8351e97b115"],"x":"A plan that meets the recommendations has at least 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, vigorous activity on at least 3 days, muscle-strengthening on at least 3 days and bone-strengthening on at least 3 days. Light activity does not count toward the daily minutes."},"a-graph":{"a":"In 2023, male students were nearly twice as likely as female students to meet the daily activity guideline. | In 2023, about 1 in 4 high school students were active for 60 minutes every day of the week. | The overall percentage of students meeting the guideline was not higher in 2023 than in 2013.","d":"activity","c":"activity-data","k":"interpret graph","df":2,"y":"multi","qt":"graph interpretation","p":2,"m":"m3","s":"a-graph","sl":"a-graph","mj":0,"h":["0ba305119a2eb2281046"],"x":"The graph shows 32% of males versus 17% of females in 2023 (nearly twice), about 25% overall in 2023 (1 in 4), and 27% in 2013, so the overall percentage did not rise. The graph cannot explain why differences exist or show what the guideline should be."},"m-spots-a":{"a":"Before and after photos | Headline claim | Personal testimonial | Seal or \"formula\" badge | Discount code | Hashtag list and its last tag | Missing information","d":"marketing","c":"marketing-tech","k":"identify","df":3,"y":"spots","qt":"identify-the-misleading-claim (clickable post)","p":3,"m":"m4","s":"mkt-a","sl":"m-spots","mj":1,"h":["28809387b1c0d9f92c6e"],"x":"Before/after photos, a dramatic headline claim, a personal testimonial, a vague \"inspired\" badge, a discount code (a financial tie), a disclosure buried in a hashtag list, and missing ingredient and research information are all red flags. The account name with its follower count and the product photo are ordinary parts of a post and are not evidence either way."},"m-match-a":{"a":"Photos labeled \"Day 1\" and \"Day 21\" taken in different lighting and poses → Before/after imagery | \"I tried it for one week and my whole team noticed!\" → Testimonial (a personal story, not evidence) | \"DETOX your body and get 300% more energy in 3 days!\" → Unrealistic claim | The tag #sp placed last in a list of ten hashtags → Weak sponsorship disclosure","d":"marketing","c":"marketing-tech","k":"classify","df":2,"y":"match","qt":"matching","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"mkt-a","sl":"m-match","mj":1,"h":["3fbcb447bd4a489002e8"],"x":"Photos at two points in time are before/after imagery. A personal quote is a testimonial. A promise of a huge change in days is an unrealistic claim. A sponsorship tag hidden at the end of many hashtags is a weak disclosure. No expert endorsement appears in this post."},"m-seq-a":{"a":"1. Stop before clicking, buying or sharing, and notice how the post is trying to make you feel. | 2. Ask who is paying for the post, what evidence it shows, and what is missing. | 3. Check what the CDC, FDA, a registered dietitian or peer-reviewed research say about the claim. | 4. Choose to skip it, share it, or look for more information, based on what the evidence shows.","d":"marketing","c":"pqvd","k":"sequence","df":2,"y":"seq","qt":"ranking: PAUSE-QUESTION-VERIFY-DECIDE","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"mkt-a","sl":"m-seq","mj":1,"h":["0bb0a0b5e5c06d7fe703"],"x":"PAUSE first (stop and notice your reaction), then QUESTION (who benefits, what evidence, what is missing), then VERIFY (credible sources), and finally DECIDE based on the evidence."},"m-spots-b":{"a":"Before and after photos | Headline claim | Personal testimonial | Seal or \"formula\" badge | Discount code | Hashtag list and its last tag | Missing information","d":"marketing","c":"marketing-tech","k":"identify","df":3,"y":"spots","qt":"identify-the-misleading-claim (clickable post)","p":3,"m":"m4","s":"mkt-b","sl":"m-spots","mj":1,"h":["ad33311d42a520e9f4fd"],"x":"Before/after photos, a dramatic headline claim, a personal testimonial, a vague \"inspired\" badge, a discount code (a financial tie), a disclosure buried in a hashtag list, and missing ingredient and research information are all red flags. The account name with its follower count and the product photo are ordinary parts of a post and are not evidence either way."},"m-match-b":{"a":"Report-card photos labeled \"Before\" and \"After 14 days\" → Before/after imagery | \"My grades went up a whole letter in one month!\" → Testimonial (a personal story, not evidence) | \"Cure brain fog and boost your focus 300% in 3 days!\" → Unrealistic claim | The tag #partner placed last in a list of ten hashtags → Weak sponsorship disclosure","d":"marketing","c":"marketing-tech","k":"classify","df":2,"y":"match","qt":"matching","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"mkt-b","sl":"m-match","mj":1,"h":["fb69eaee7834778732b5"],"x":"Photos at two points in time are before/after imagery. A personal quote is a testimonial. A promise of a huge change in days is an unrealistic claim. A sponsorship tag hidden at the end of many hashtags is a weak disclosure. No expert endorsement appears in this post."},"m-seq-b":{"a":"1. Stop before clicking, buying or sharing, and notice how the post is trying to make you feel. | 2. Ask who is paying for the post, what evidence it shows, and what is missing. | 3. Check what the CDC, FDA, a registered dietitian or peer-reviewed research say about the claim. | 4. Choose to skip it, share it, or look for more information, based on what the evidence shows.","d":"marketing","c":"pqvd","k":"sequence","df":2,"y":"seq","qt":"ranking: PAUSE-QUESTION-VERIFY-DECIDE","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"mkt-b","sl":"m-seq","mj":1,"h":["7db7799786874aa0e165"],"x":"PAUSE first (stop and notice your reaction), then QUESTION (who benefits, what evidence, what is missing), then VERIFY (credible sources), and finally DECIDE based on the evidence."},"m-halo":{"a":"Positive claims about one feature (organic, gluten-free) can make the whole product seem healthy, even though the label shows 14 g of added sugars (28% DV).","d":"marketing","c":"health-halo","k":"explain","df":2,"y":"mc","qt":"package analysis","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"m-halo","sl":"m-halo","mj":1,"h":["775d0542f15b7fa3f591"],"x":"The health halo effect is when a positive claim about one feature leads people to assume the whole product is healthy. The label shows the product has 14 g of added sugars (28% DV) and no fiber, so the claims do not tell the whole story.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"m-sources":{"a":"A CDC or NIH web page summarizing research on the topic → More credible | A peer-reviewed study with a control group, published in a medical journal → More credible | A registered dietitian explaining the research and linking sources → More credible | The supplement company's own website listing customer testimonials → Less credible | An influencer's affiliate-link post with a discount code → Less credible | Five-star reviews on the product's sales page → Less credible","d":"marketing","c":"source-credibility","k":"evaluate","df":2,"y":"sort","qt":"source credibility sort","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"m-sources","sl":"m-sources","mj":0,"h":["9dcee9dd0700c141667b"],"x":"Government health agencies, peer-reviewed research and registered dietitians who cite sources are credible. A company's own website, a paid affiliate post and reviews on a sales page all have a financial interest or are anecdotes, not evidence."},"m-study":{"a":"It suggests posts showing unhealthy snacks can raise how much children eat right afterward in a lab, but it does not prove long-term effects for all teens.","d":"marketing","c":"influence-evidence","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"mc","qt":"data-table interpretation","p":2,"m":"m4","s":"m-study","sl":"m-study","mj":0,"h":["8e254b33e61c83dc4551"],"x":"The experiment measured immediate snack intake in one lab setting for 9- to 11-year-olds. It supports a careful conclusion about short-term effects of unhealthy-snack posts, but it cannot prove long-term effects, weight change, or results for all ages.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"g-match":{"a":"\"I will add one serving of vegetables to dinner more often during the next 4 weeks.\" → Measurable | \"I will run a full marathon next weekend, even though I have never run more than a mile.\" → Achievable | \"I will drink water instead of soda at lunch on every school day.\" → Time-bound | \"I will practice free throws 20 minutes a day, 3 days a week, for 5 weeks, even though I have no interest in basketball.\" → Relevant","d":"goals","c":"smart-components","k":"diagnose","df":2,"y":"match","qt":"matching","p":2,"m":"m5","s":"g-match","sl":"g-match","mj":0,"h":["33b79585df18a8ff5df4"],"x":"\"More often\" is not measurable. A marathon next weekend is not achievable for someone who has never run more than a mile. The water goal has no end date, so it is not time-bound. Practicing free throws with no interest in basketball is not relevant to the person's own priorities."},"g-repair-act":{"a":"WHAT will {N1} do?: Take a 20-minute brisk walk after school | HOW MUCH or HOW OFTEN?: on 4 days each week | WHY does it matter to {N1}?: because I want more energy for practice and to feel less stressed | FOR HOW LONG?: for the next 6 weeks, with a check-in at week 3","d":"goals","c":"goal-repair","k":"create","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"SMART-goal repair challenge","p":3,"m":"m5","s":"g-repair-act","sl":"g-repair","mj":1,"h":["607365cfc7015dffcab8"],"x":"The repaired goal names a specific action, uses a number that is achievable (4 days), gives a personal reason, and sets an end point with a check-in."},"g-repair-veg":{"a":"WHAT will {N1} do?: Add one vegetable to dinner | HOW MUCH or HOW OFTEN?: on 5 nights each week | WHY does it matter to {N1}?: because vegetables add fiber and vitamins that support my energy | FOR HOW LONG?: for the next 4 weeks, then review","d":"goals","c":"goal-repair","k":"create","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"SMART-goal repair challenge","p":3,"m":"m5","s":"g-repair-veg","sl":"g-repair","mj":1,"h":["aba2e826cfd626bdc362"],"x":"The repaired goal is a specific behavior, the target (5 nights) is measurable and achievable, the reason is personal, and the 4-week review makes it time-bound."},"g-repair-sleep":{"a":"WHAT will {N1} do?: Start my bedtime routine at 9:45 p.m. with screens off | HOW MUCH or HOW OFTEN?: on school nights, aiming for 8 to 10 hours | WHY does it matter to {N1}?: because enough sleep helps me concentrate and recover from practice | FOR HOW LONG?: for the next 3 weeks, checking my progress each Sunday","d":"goals","c":"goal-repair","k":"create","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"SMART-goal repair challenge","p":3,"m":"m5","s":"g-repair-sleep","sl":"g-repair","mj":1,"h":["8879a2b276e528119a3b"],"x":"The repaired goal names a specific routine, a measurable and achievable target, a personal reason, and a 3-week time frame with a weekly check."},"g-barrier":{"a":"No time to cook after practice → Prepare ingredients or cook a big batch on the weekend and keep quick backup options | Fresh produce seems expensive → Buy frozen or canned vegetables (no added salt) or produce that is in season | Friends do not want to be active → Invite a friend to join, or join a team or class to meet others who are active | Keeps forgetting the new habit → Set a phone reminder and tie the habit to something already in the routine","d":"goals","c":"barriers-strategies","k":"match","df":2,"y":"match","qt":"matching","p":2,"m":"m5","s":"g-barrier","sl":"g-barrier","mj":0,"h":["97c9e942068cf4f2d0fc"],"x":"Batch preparation saves time, frozen and canned vegetables lower cost, a friend or team provides social support, and reminders tied to routines help with forgetting. Waiting for motivation does not address any of the barriers."},"g-eval":{"a":"I will jog and walk in intervals for 20 minutes, 3 days a week, for 6 weeks so I have more stamina for tryouts.","d":"goals","c":"goal-eval","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"mc","qt":"scenario multiple choice","p":2,"m":"m5","s":"g-eval","sl":"g-eval","mj":0,"h":["ed751821f0d5099e82cf"],"x":"Choice A is specific, measurable (20 minutes, 3 days), achievable for a beginner, relevant to tryouts, and time-bound (6 weeks). The others are unrealistic, vague, or based on trends rather than {N1}'s own purpose.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"g-plan":{"a":"1. Choose one specific behavior to change | 2. Set a measurable target and a deadline | 3. Predict barriers that could get in the way | 4. Plan strategies for each barrier | 5. Track progress, then review and adjust","d":"goals","c":"goal-process","k":"sequence","df":2,"y":"seq","qt":"ranking","p":1,"m":"m5","s":"g-plan","sl":"g-plan","mj":0,"h":["3253fdc4ff9a02dc949f"],"x":"Start with one specific behavior, set a measurable target and deadline, predict barriers, plan strategies for them, and then track progress and adjust."},"s-def":{"a":"Food environment → The foods that are available, affordable and marketed where a person lives, learns and shops | Consolidation → A few large companies controlling a growing share of a part of the food system, such as processing | Food insecurity → A household's limited or uncertain access to enough food because of lack of money or other resources | Resilience → A system's ability to keep supplying food and recover when something disrupts it","d":"systems","c":"food-vocab","k":"define","df":1,"y":"match","qt":"matching","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"s-def","sl":"s-def","mj":0,"h":["4ac8fa849ef083d6f5f8"],"x":"The food environment is what is available, affordable and marketed around you. Consolidation is when a few companies control more of a market. Food insecurity is a household condition tied to resources. Resilience is the ability to keep supplying food and recover from disruption."},"s-network":{"a":"Farm subsidies and crop-insurance incentives → large supply of corn and soy | Large supply of corn and soy → cheap ingredients for processed foods | Cheap ingredients → lower prices for many ultra-processed foods | Heavy marketing of processed foods → higher consumption of the advertised foods | Few large processing plants → one closure causes a large supply disruption | Few full-service grocery stores nearby → less fresh produce available near home","d":"systems","c":"causal-chain","k":"map causes","df":3,"y":"multi","qt":"cause-and-effect mapping","p":3,"m":"m6","s":"s-network","sl":"s-network","mj":1,"h":["f92af4131f31c026666f"],"x":"Incentives influence what is grown; a large supply of commodity crops lowers ingredient cost and food prices; marketing raises consumption; fewer processing plants make one closure matter more; and few nearby stores reduce nearby fresh produce. The other arrows reverse the direction or have no direct mechanism."},"s-tradeoff":{"a":"More consolidation lowered cost per unit but also increased the share of supply lost when one large plant closed, a trade-off between efficiency and resilience.","d":"systems","c":"resilience-efficiency","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"mc","qt":"simulation: trade-off analysis","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"foodsys","sl":"s-tradeoff","mj":1,"h":["1f62e12c61bf9d3bdf9b"],"x":"In the model, fewer, larger plants lowered cost per unit (efficiency) but one closure removed a larger share of supply (less resilience). The lab shows a trade-off; it does not prove consolidation is always bad.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"s-lever":{"a":"9","d":"systems","c":"resilience-efficiency","k":"apply","df":2,"y":"slider","qt":"simulation: slider","p":1,"m":"m6","s":"foodsys","sl":"s-lever","mj":0,"h":["857de1d126b21aded587"],"x":"At level 9 there are 4 equal plants, so closing one removes 25% of supply. At lower levels there are more plants and each closure removes less."},"s-access":{"a":"Area B: dense apartments on a bus line","d":"systems","c":"food-access","k":"analyze map","df":3,"y":"hotspot","qt":"clickable map","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"s-access","sl":"s-access","mj":1,"h":["baf803e9a8cd163d134b"],"x":"Area B has dense housing, a high share of households without a car, and a bus line, and it is far from the existing supermarket. Area A is already served, C and D have few homes, and E has high car ownership.","o":["A","B","C","D","E"]},"s-insecurity":{"a":"The share of U.S. households that were food insecure was higher in 2023 than in 2021. | In 2023, about 1 in 7 U.S. households were food insecure. | The graph measures households, not individual people.","d":"systems","c":"food-insecurity-data","k":"interpret graph","df":2,"y":"multi","qt":"graph interpretation","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"s-insecurity","sl":"s-insecurity","mj":0,"h":["27ceacd8c0620d579d31"],"x":"The bars show 10.2% in 2021, 12.8% in 2022 and 13.5% in 2023, so the share rose, and 13.5% is about 1 in 7 households. The data are for households. The graph cannot show causes such as food choices or prices."},"s-upf":{"a":"Ultra-processed foods supplied about two-thirds of U.S. youth calories in 2018, so their availability, price and marketing are worth examining, though the data alone do not show that any one food causes a health outcome.","d":"systems","c":"ultra-processed","k":"interpret graph","df":3,"y":"mc","qt":"graph interpretation","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"s-upf","sl":"s-upf","mj":0,"h":["84c06e7de1e6f8903861"],"x":"The graph shows trend data: ultra-processed foods rose from about 61% to 67% of youth calories. This invites questions about the food environment, but trend data cannot show one food causes an outcome, cannot reduce the pattern to willpower, and cannot label every food in the category the same.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"s-doc":{"a":"Find the original data (for example, USDA reports on how much cattle purchasing is handled by the largest packers), check what was measured and when, and remember the film has a point of view.","d":"systems","c":"evidence-docs","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"mc","qt":"evidence evaluation","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"s-doc","sl":"s-doc","mj":0,"h":["e46bbcb0440fb60bbae6"],"x":"Documentaries persuade with a point of view and can contain accurate and inaccurate parts. Checking the original data, what it measures and when is the strongest approach. USDA reports that the four largest packers handled about 85% of steer and heifer purchases (data from 2015), but exact figures depend on what is measured.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"s-tech":{"a":"What does independent research show about environmental impacts across the whole life cycle, including energy use? | Has a food-safety agency reviewed the product, and how does its nutrition compare with the foods it replaces? | Who paid for the studies, and what would the cost and scale be compared with current options?","d":"systems","c":"emerging-tech","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"multi","qt":"evidence evaluation (multi-select)","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"s-tech","sl":"s-tech","mj":0,"h":["8c0fb716f286aec0bbf5"],"x":"Life-cycle research, safety and nutrition review, and funding and scale are evidence questions. Follower counts, names and celebrity posts say nothing about whether the claim is true."},"s-regen":{"a":"Planting cover crops between harvests → Keeps soil covered, reducing erosion and adding organic matter | Rotating grazing animals among pastures → Gives plants time to regrow, which supports healthier grass and soil | Reducing tillage (plowing) → Protects soil structure and helps keep carbon in the soil | Growing a diversity of crops → Supports more resilient farms and can reduce pest outbreaks","d":"systems","c":"regen-ag","k":"match","df":2,"y":"match","qt":"matching","p":2,"m":"m6","s":"s-regen","sl":"s-regen","mj":0,"h":["e6a8dba096f3f8d4db78"],"x":"Cover crops protect soil, rotational grazing lets plants recover, reduced tillage protects soil structure, and crop diversity supports resilience. Benefits vary by place and take time, and no practice guarantees lower prices."},"s-workers":{"a":"Stronger safety standards and enforcement in processing plants, including paid sick leave","d":"systems","c":"workers-policy","k":"apply","df":2,"y":"mc","qt":"scenario multiple choice","p":1,"m":"m6","s":"s-workers","sl":"s-workers","mj":0,"h":["0fc7ccf84c2e06a238bf"],"x":"Safety standards, enforcement and paid sick leave directly protect workers, and healthier workers also keep plants running. The other choices do not address worker health.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"i-fuel-1":{"a":"Best snack for {N1}: Protein Crunch Bar | Strongest reason: The Protein Crunch Bar has 10 g protein and 5 g added sugars, matching both requirements.","d":"integrated","c":"int-snack","k":"integrate","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"integrated scenario: labels + claims","p":2,"m":"m7","s":"i-fuel-1","sl":"i-fuel","mj":1,"h":["f8b5168ebe371b11423e"],"x":"Only the Protein Crunch Bar meets both the 8 g protein and the 10 g added-sugar limits. Front claims (real fruit, only 110 calories) do not show whether the need is met."},"i-fuel-2":{"a":"Best snack for {N1}: Banana Oat Cup | Strongest reason: The Banana Oat Cup is the only one with enough energy for the need while staying within the 150 mg sodium limit (90 mg).","d":"integrated","c":"int-snack","k":"integrate","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"integrated scenario: labels + claims","p":2,"m":"m7","s":"i-fuel-2","sl":"i-fuel","mj":1,"h":["7d04ccefd95fc269630c"],"x":"The Banana Oat Cup supplies the energy (210 calories) and keeps sodium low (90 mg). The Zero Sugar Fizz Bar is high in protein but has only 70 calories; the puffs exceed the sodium limit."},"i-move-1":{"a":"Best change: Add 30 minutes of brisk walking or a bodyweight circuit on Tuesday, Thursday and the weekend | Strongest reason: It adds moderate-to-vigorous minutes on the empty days and can include muscle-strengthening, addressing both gaps.","d":"integrated","c":"int-plan","k":"integrate","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"integrated scenario: FITT adjustment","p":2,"m":"m7","s":"i-move-1","sl":"i-move","mj":1,"h":["4a7dc90c2e4ccac0198d"],"x":"Jordan has vigorous days but no activity on four days and no muscle-strengthening. Adding 30-minute moderate sessions on the empty days, including bodyweight work, increases Frequency and changes Type."},"i-move-2":{"a":"Best change: Walk faster or add hills until talking is possible but singing is not, lengthen some walks toward 60 minutes, and add a bodyweight circuit on three days | Strongest reason: It raises intensity and time toward 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity and adds a muscle-strengthening type.","d":"integrated","c":"int-plan","k":"integrate","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"integrated scenario: FITT adjustment","p":2,"m":"m7","s":"i-move-2","sl":"i-move","mj":1,"h":["9a63b35286934edf42b5"],"x":"Being able to sing means the walks are light intensity, which does not count toward the daily moderate-to-vigorous minutes. Raising the intensity, lengthening some sessions and adding muscle-strengthening closes the gaps."},"i-env":{"a":"Personal choices matter, and they are also shaped by what is available, affordable, convenient and marketed nearby, so both individual and system-level changes can help.","d":"integrated","c":"int-env","k":"evaluate","df":3,"y":"mc","qt":"integrated scenario: individual vs systemic","p":2,"m":"m7","s":"i-env","sl":"i-env","mj":1,"h":["7a50815ea7e6dcfeb3ae"],"x":"Health choices are made by individuals inside a food environment. Availability, price, convenience and marketing all shape what is easy to choose, so individual strategies and system-level changes both matter.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"i-claim":{"a":"Wait before buying, ask who profits and what evidence is shown, check the claim with the FDA, CDC or a registered dietitian, then decide using that evidence.","d":"integrated","c":"int-claims","k":"integrate","df":3,"y":"mc","qt":"integrated scenario: PQVD","p":2,"m":"m7","s":"i-claim","sl":"i-claim","mj":1,"h":["7059023614e2e9954be6"],"x":"The strongest plan pauses, questions who profits and what evidence exists, verifies with credible sources, and decides from evidence. Buying first, rejecting everything, or relying on popularity skips key steps.","o":["a","b","c","d"]},"i-goal":{"a":"The SMART goal: Eat one serving of vegetables with dinner on 4 nights a week and walk briskly for 20 minutes after school on 3 days a week, for the next 4 weeks | A strategy for the access barrier: Buy frozen or canned vegetables (no added salt) from the corner store or a monthly bus trip, and keep them stocked | How Jordan checks a new claim about a \"miracle\" supplement: Pause, ask who profits, and verify with credible sources such as the FDA or a registered dietitian | How Jordan tracks progress: A weekly check of nights with vegetables and days walked, then adjust the plan","d":"integrated","c":"int-goal","k":"create","df":3,"y":"slots","qt":"integrated scenario: build a plan","p":3,"m":"m7","s":"i-goal","sl":"i-goal","mj":1,"h":["1d34dda0816c3f81f947"],"x":"The strongest plan is specific, measurable, achievable and time-bound; it works around the access barrier with frozen or canned vegetables; it uses PAUSE, QUESTION, VERIFY, DECIDE for new claims; and it tracks progress weekly."}};
